AB  - High-intensity mining activities in open-pit mining areas can easily induce significant ground subsidence and disturbances in the distribution of surface features. The coupling relationship between these two factors directly impacts mine safety and ecological and environmental management. This study takes the Pingshuo Mining Area in Shanxi Province as the study area. Time-series InSAR techniques, specifically SBAS-InSAR and PS-InSAR, were comprehensively employed to monitor ground subsidence with comparative analysis of the results, revealing the spatiotemporal evolution characteristics of subsidence in the mining area. Additionally, by integrating the distribution of surface elements in the mining area obtained from high-resolution remote sensing imagery, a coupling relationship between surface elements and subsidence was established. The obtained results reveal that the deformation rates in the study area range from $\mathbf{- 2 7 4. 2 8 ~ m m} / \mathbf{y r}$ to $\mathbf{4 9. 1 3 ~ m m} / \mathbf{y r}$, with SBAS-InSAR demonstrating better adaptability in low-coherence regions. Coupling analysis revealed that subsidence areas are highly correlated with the spatial distribution of typical surface elements such as mine pits and waste dumps, reflecting the significant impact of mining activities on surface deformation. This research provides valuable insights for geological environment monitoring and the development of green mines in open-pit mining areas.
