AB  - This study addresses the monitoring of water quality in mining regions, focusing on arsenic (As) concentration as a critical parameter and total suspended solids (TSS) as its proxy. We investigate the integration of multi-source and multi-scale Earth Observation (EO) techniques by combining diverse spectral and spatial resolutions from several platforms: a small-scale OceanOptics STS-VIS spectroradiometer mounted on a DJI Phantom 3 UAV; a Parrot Sequoia 4-band multispectral camera deployed on a DJI Phantom 4 Pro UAV; and 8-band multispectral satellite imagery from Planet Labs with a spatial resolution of 3 meters. The spectral datasets were analyzed alongside in situ surface water samples collected during UAV-flight campaigns and from long-term monitoring conducted by Hellas Gold (HG). The findings highlight the potential of synergistic use of these datasets for improved water quality assessment in mining-impacted environments. Future research will focus on refining the temporal analysis and developing predictive models using the complete PlanetScope time series in combination with HG’s open-access long-term monitoring data.
