AB  - As one of the main industrial wastes generated by mining activities, tailings ponds not only occupy a large amount of land resources, but also have the potential risk of causing environmental pollution and geological disasters. Therefore, achieving automatic identification of tailings ponds is vital for resource supervision and ecological protection. However, due to the inherent morphological and textural complexity of tailings ponds in remote sensing images, existing general-purpose object detectors struggle to achieve accurate detection. To meet these challenges, this letter proposes a tailings ponds detection framework based on irregular attention features, which is targeted at both positioning and classification. Specifically, this letter introduces a context-aware location refinement module, which uses position encoding to serialize images to improve the positioning accuracy of irregular targets; and a part attention classification refinement module is designed to focus on the most discriminative local areas to achieve more effective texture feature extraction and classification performance improvement. For evaluation purposes, we constructed a remote sensing dataset containing tailings ponds and buildings. Compared with existing benchmark methods, our method achieves satisfactory performance in the tailings ponds detection task, providing a reliable technical support for efficient and automated monitoring of tailings ponds.
KW  - Attention mechanism
KW  - context-aware
KW  - remote sensing image
KW  - tailings ponds detection
