AB  - The multi-source remote sensing images in mountainous areas are affected by variations in resolution, imaging angles, and atmospheric conditions, resulting in significant nonlinear radiation and geometric distortion discrepancies among the images, which pose a huge challenge to image matching. This article introduces a multi-source remote sensing image matching method specifically for mountainous areas based on spatial structural features. By mining stable structural information and combining multi-scale feature expression with log-polar coordinate measurement, the accuracy of the matching algorithm is improved. Firstly, we calculate the phase consistency structure map and use bilateral filtering to extract spatial structural features from the multi-source remote sensing images. Secondly, a nonlinear scale space is constructed using a nonlinear diffusion function, and a nonlinear weighted moment map is generated using the Log-Gabor filtering. The Harris algorithm is utilized to detect feature points within the nonlinear weighted moment map. Subsequently, the phase consistency direction is extended to generate absolute phase consistency direction features, and then the log-polar coordinate description framework is combined to calculate the feature vectors. Finally, the bilateral matching method is applied to obtain the initial feature correspondence, and the fast sample consensus algorithm is used to eliminate mismatches and achieve high-precision feature correspondence. The experimental results show that our proposed method significantly enhances both the number of correct matches as well as the correct matching rate and overall matching accuracy when compared with traditional methods, providing effective technical support for applications such as remote sensing image fusion and change detection in mountainous areas.
