AB  - Large-scale open-pit mining alters vegetation cover, exposed-surface conditions, and land surface temperature, requiring repeatable post-mine monitoring beyond visual assessment. This study applies a time-series remote-sensing framework to evaluate surface-condition trajectories at the Aruwakkalu Limestone Quarry from 2015 to 2025. Landsat 8/9 data were analyzed across three fixed zones: mining footprint, rehabilitated area, and reference forest. Four indicators were derived: Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), Normalized Difference Moisture Index (NDMI), Bare Soil Index (BSI), and Land Surface Temperature (LST). The mining footprint showed clear spectral degradation, with NDVI declining to 0.166, NDMI to -0.095, BSI increasing to 0.197, and annual median LST peaking at 40.84°C. In contrast, the rehabilitated area showed statistically consistent spectral recovery: NDVI increased to 0.623, NDMI to 0.206, and BSI declined to -0.136. Although rehabilitated-area LST declined to 32.30°C by 2025, its temporal trend was not statistically significant and remained 2.21°C warmer than the reference forest. The findings support progressive spectral recovery, while field validation is required to confirm ecological or hydrological restoration.
