AB  - Multispectral imaging (MSI) based on uncrewed aerial vehicle (UAV) can capture valuable spectral, thereby improving the capability of object tracking. However, most existing multispectral (MS) trackers focus on feature matching of the foreground target, leading to target loss due to sparse features and strong interference under complex backgrounds. To address this challenge, we propose the positive–negative prompt mining network (PNPM-Net), which models both background interference and target features as structured negative and positive prompts to enhance feature discriminability under complex scenarios. It mainly consists of three key components: spatial–spectral–temporal attention mining (SSTAM), positive–negative joint mining (PNJM) module, and dual-incentive prompt modulation (DIPM) module. First, we propose SSTAM for adaptive spectral channel reweighting and spatial reshaping, thereby augmenting the spatial–spectral separability between target and background signatures under complex dynamic scenarios. Second, we introduce PNJM to generate contrastive priors modeling the positive and negative prompts. Finally, we develop DIPM to employ these priors for feature enhancement and background suppression, thereby increasing the interclass discriminativeness between target and background. Experiments show PNPM-Net achieves 71.8% AUC and 88.1% DP@20, surpassing state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods by 18.5% and 9.2%, respectively, which demonstrate the strong potential in UAV-based MS object tracking (MOT) area.
