AB  - In remote sensing (RS) scenarios, there is a domain gap between existing pre-trained tasks and oriented object detection (OOD) tasks. It leads to misaligned prior knowledge in the model, deviates from the fine-tuning optimization goal, and causes fuzzy localization and error classification (missed detections, false alarms, and multi-recognition). The lack of instance-level supervision and of mining object characteristics is the primary reason for the poor transfer performance of existing methods on RS OOD tasks. To address this problem, we propose a pre-training method specifically designed for RS OOD tasks. Specifically, it comprises two components: a General Localization Representation (GLR) branch and an Object-aware Region Reconstruction (ORR) branch. GLR includes two algorithms that convert sparsely represented oriented bounding boxes into densely distributed Gaussian kernel representations and adaptively distribute them to the multi-scale sample space. It eliminates complex, diverse sample-assignment strategies and enables general localization learning. ORR adaptively controls the size and distribution of masked regions based on the target’s geometric characteristics. It guides the model to efficiently acquire universal feature representations that are strongly correlated with targets in RS scenarios. In experiments, we validate its performance on the RS OOD tasks using four encoder architectures (convolutional and Transformer families). With fewer data, it outperforms existing supervised and self-supervised methods, achieving state-of-the-art performance. Furthermore, comprehensive ablation studies validate LOBA’s strong transfer generalization and stable performance.
