AB  - Efficient bioprocess monitoring requires rapid and precise measurements across multiple parameters. Realizing sensors capable of providing this functionality in a cost-effective, scalable way has been challenging. Achieving high throughput measurements in complex biological media is one of the top challenges which remains. In this work a hyperspectral imaging (HSI) platform is used to spatially characterize the response from a nanophotonic biosensor. The sensor consists of nanopillars fabricated by nanoimprint lithography. These structures have a plasmonic resonance that can be excited directly by light in the visible wavelength range. The technique developed can acquire thousands of spectra from images of nanostructured areas and determine regions with high quality measurement capabilities. By altering the bulk and local refractive index (RI) around the plasmonic nanostructures we demonstrate large areas of uniformly high sensitivity. A sensitivity of 187.2 nm/RI with a standard deviation of 11.5 nm/RI was achieved over a 15 mm2biosensor surface. We also demonstrate a highly surface sensitive response thereby reducing bulk effects in biological media. These results show that this platform provides a way to perform multiplexed analysis in a cost-effective scalable way, bringing high throughput, sensitive measurements to bioprocessing.
