TI  - Adaptive Inspection Framework for Gold Finger Modules Using Multi-Color Illumination and Optimized Algorithms
JO  - 2026 Pan Pacific Strategic Electronics Symposium (Pan Pacific)
PY  - 2026
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.23919/PanPacific70217.2026.11413899
AB  - Accurate inspection of gold fingers in electronic modules is critical for ensuring product integrity and manufacturing reliability in high-volume production environments. Despite advances in automated optical inspection (AOI) technology, persistent false calls stemming from algorithmic limitations continue to compromise inspection accuracy, reduce throughput, and increase manufacturing costs. These false calls not only disrupt production workflows but also undermine confidence in automated inspection systems, necessitating excessive manual verification and rework. This paper investigates the root causes of false calls associated with conventional inspection algorithms-including Contour Match and Brightness Statistics-and proposes advanced solutions to enhance precision, repeatability, and overall system performance. A comprehensive analysis of inspection failures across multiple production lines identified three primary contributors to false calls. First, inconsistent gold finger geometries arising from manufacturing tolerances create significant challenges for rigid algorithmic approaches that rely on fixed template matching. Second, imprecise inspection window definitions fail to account for component-to-component variations and positioning tolerances, leading to boundary detection errors and incomplete feature capture. Third, difficulties in differentiating reflective materialsparticularly distinguishing gold surfaces from other metallic finishes under varying lighting conditions-result in misclassification of acceptable components as defective. To address these fundamental limitations, a novel adaptive inspection algorithm was developed that dynamically adjusts window sizing and positioning based on real-time analysis of component geometry. This adaptive approach eliminates the rigid constraints of conventional methods, enabling robust inspection across a wide range of geometric variations while maintaining stringent quality standards. The algorithm incorporates intelligent feature recognition that identifies gold finger boundaries with sub-pixel accuracy, ensuring consistent performance regardless of manufacturing tolerances or component placement variations. Additionally, an innovative multi-color illumination system was introduced to overcome the material differentiation challenges inherent in single-wavelength inspection. By utilizing RGB value mapping and spectral analysis, the system exploits the distinct reflective properties of different metallic surfaces across multiple wavelengths. This multi-spectral approach significantly improves material differentiation, enhances edge recognition, and provides robust performance under varying environmental conditions. The illumination system integrates seamlessly with existing AOI hardware, requiring minimal modifications to current production setups. Extensive validation was conducted on production hardware across multiple manufacturing facilities, processing thousands of inspection cycles under real-world conditions. Results demonstrated substantial reductions in false call rates-exceeding 75% improvement over baseline performance-while maintaining detection accuracy above 99% for genuine defects. Statistical analysis confirmed the robustness and repeatability of the proposed methods across different operators, shifts, and production batches. The approach also reduced inspection cycle time by eliminating unnecessary manual verification steps, directly contributing to improved manufacturing throughput and reduced operational costs. The proposed methodology establishes a scalable framework for intelligent inspection that can be extended to other critical features in electronic assembly processes. This work represents a meaningful advancement in precision-driven quality assurance, delivering enhanced consistency, accuracy, and reliability for gold finger inspection while demonstrating the potential for adaptive algorithms and multi-spectral imaging in next-generation AOI systems.
