TI  - A Method for Retrieving Surface Displacement by Integrating Simulated Ascending and Descending InSAR Observations
JO  - 2026 International Conference on Computational Intelligence and Machine Learning (CIML)
PY  - 2026
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/CIML69155.2026.11692635
AB  - To overcome the limitation of applying integrated ascending-descending Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR) caused by missing descending orbit data, this study proposes a data-driven model that combines ridge regression with Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) time series analysis. The objective is to retrieve high-precision surface subsidence measurements using ascending track data alone. Taking the 150202 working face of a mining area as the test site, historical ascending Small Baseline Subset InSAR (SBAS-InSAR)-derived subsidence data and their corresponding fused results were used as training samples. Ridge regression was first used to learn a stable mapping from ascending-orbit SBAS-InSAR results to the corresponding ascending–descending fused deformation and produce an initial prediction. Then, the residuals of ridge regression were modeled and corrected using an ARIMA time-series model to capture temporal autocorrelation and reduce systematic errors. The final simulated fusion result was obtained by adding the ARIMA-predicted residuals to the ridge-regression outputs.Experimental results demonstrate that the simulated fusion results show only minor deviations from the actual fused outcomes.Validation with leveling data confirms that the proposed approach significantly improves accuracy compared to using ascending-only data and meets the precision requirements for monitoring small-gradient subsidence in mining areas. This study provides an effective technical solution for obtaining high-precision InSAR-based subsidence monitoring in the absence of descending orbit data. The achieved precision of less than 10 mm demonstrates the method's capability to reliably delineate mining subsidence boundaries. The proposed ridge regression-ARIMA framework offers a practical and data-efficient alternative to complex deep learning models, showing significant potential for application in mining areas with limited data acquisition capabilities.This study provides an effective technical solution for obtaining high-precision InSAR-based subsidence monitoring in the absence of descending orbit data.
