DO  - 10.1109/InGARSS67683.2025.11583839
AB  - Soil Organic Carbon (SOC) served as a vital indicator of soil health, influencing its physical, chemical, and biological functions. Conventional chemical methods for SOC estimation, although accurate, are laborious, time-consuming, and environmentally demanding when applied to large datasets. Hyperspectral imaging (HSI) has emerged as a rapid, nondestructive, and cost-effective alternative that captures both spatial and spectral information. This study evaluates the potential of laboratory-based Shortwave Infrared (SWIR) HSI $\boldsymbol{(} \mathbf{9 3 5}-\mathbf{1 7 2 0} \mathbf{~ n m} \boldsymbol{)}$ for predicting SOC in Indian soils using several machine learning models, including Support Vector Regression (SVR), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), XGBoost, Bagging, and Ridge Regression. Among these, SVR achieved the best performance (validation $\mathbf{R}^{\mathbf{2}} {=} \mathbf{0. 7 9} {;} \mathbf{R M S E} {= 0. 2 5 \%} \mathbf{S O C}$). Feature importance analysis identified SOC-sensitive spectral ranges between $\mathbf{1 3 0 0 - 1 7 0 0 ~ n m}$, associated with $\mathbf{C - H}, \mathbf{N - H}$, and $\mathbf{O}-\mathbf{H}$ absorptions from organic matter and clay minerals. Spatial interpolation using simple kriging revealed strong agreement between laboratory-measured and SVR-predicted SOC across the West Bengal region. The study demonstrates that laboratory-based HSI combined with ML models can accurately estimate and map SOC, thereby supporting future soil monitoring and precision agriculture efforts. Future efforts should focus on extending this approach to real-time field-scale applications, integrating portable sensors and multi-source geospatial data for operational soil health monitoring.
