AB  - Existing scene classification methods are mainly based on high spatial resolution (HSR) images, which contain limited spectral information, and some complex scenes with similar visual perception (e.g., texture, shape and color), are prone to be misclassified. Hyperspectral images contain detailed spectral information that can realize fine classification of ground objects by mining subtle spectral differences, but existing data is mainly oriented to pixel-wise interpretation. Based on the above facts, we introduce a hyperspectral data set for remote sensing scene classification (HSRS-SC), which contains 1385 images divided into 5 semantic classes. Images in the HSRS-SC data set possess a spatial resolution of 1 meter and a wavelength ranging from visible light to near-infrared, to reflect the rich spatial and spectral features of ground objects. To combine the above two features, an attention-based deep feature learning network (ADFLN) is developed for scene classification of hyperspectral images. In the proposed ADFLN, spatial-spectral hybrid attention mechanism is adopted to highlight key discriminative information by using ResNet18 as backbone. Experiments performed on the HSRS-SC show that hyperspectral images achieve better classification results than RGB images. Furthermore, by introducing hybrid attention mechanism, overall classification accuracy can be further improved.
