AB  - Hyperspectral object tracking leverages rich spectral information to achieve robust target identification and localization in challenging scenes including cluttered backgrounds, camouflage, and target deformation. However, existing methods often drift or fail under appearance changes, prolonged occlusion, and frequent target re-entries. We propose a detection-driven framework where the Segment Anything Model 2 (SAM2) serves as the segmentation backbone initialized with a first-frame bounding box. When tracking quality declines or at periodic intervals, a dualdetector module (Real-Time Detection Transformer (RTDETR) + YOLOv12) generates high-confidence candidate boxes that are re-injected into SAM2 as new prompts for target re-localization. Combined with hard-example mining and two-stage training, this approach enhances detector robustness. Evaluation on the HOTC2025 dataset shows consistent performance across VIS, RedNIR, and NIR modalities, achieving AUC $=0.696$ and DP@20=0.899. The results demonstrate strong anti-drift capability and accurate boundary preservation, providing an effective solution for hyperspectral tracking.
