AB  - Summary <p>Hyperspectral remote‐sensing images contain rich spectral information, where each pixel can extract a complete and high‐resolution spectral curve. However, there are numerous mixed pixels in HSIs, i.e., the image unit is mixed with multiple pure features due to the limited spatial resolution of the image. These mixed pixels will affect the accuracy of the subsequent processing of HSIs, such as classification, target detection, and so on. Therefore, the decomposition of mixed pixels, i.e., unmixing, is crucial for the refined processing of HSIs. How to effectively use spatial–spectral information within HSIs is the key to achieving efficient unmixing. Considering that low‐rank representation (LRR) and sparse constraints are two efficient and noise‐tolerant tools for mining spatial–spectral information, this chapter offers a summary of unmixing algorithms based on LRR and sparse constraints. Additionally, it delves into the analysis and evaluation of these various algorithms, which demonstrates that incorporating both LRR and sparse constraints can significantly facilitate the purity of hyperspectral image unmixing.</p>
