AB  - Landslide represent constitute a major geohazard across China's orographic landscapes, posing severe threats to mining communities, critical infrastructure, and natural resources. To overcome the limitations of traditional manual identification and computer vision methods, we select YOLOv11 as our baseline model. Building upon its efficient architecture, we propose PKG-YOLO. First, we design the PKABlock module, this module substantially augments the network's multi-scale perception capabilities, facilitating more effective adaptation to variably-sized landslide targets amidst complex background interference. Subsequently, standard convolutional layers are replaced with GhostConv operations, significantly reducing model parameters while enhancing inference efficiency. Furthermore, this research establishes the Liulin Landslide Dataset, providing scientific value for advancing landslide mechanism comprehension and disaster management strategies. Experimental evaluations demonstrate that the proposed PKG-YOLO model achieves 93.7% precision and 92.1% recall on the Liulin benchmark while sustaining 61 FPS detection throughput. Comprehensive validation across multiple public datasets and our Liulin dataset confirms the model's optimal equilibrium between detection accuracy and computational efficiency, consistently outperforming state-of-the-art approaches.
