TI  - FedMineMT: Personalized Federated Learning with Local EMA Teachers for Robust Foreign Object Detection in Coal Mines
JO  - 2026 8th International Conference on Electronic Engineering and Informatics (EEI)
PY  - 2026
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/EEI70303.2026.11640614
AB  - Foreign object detection in coal mines naturally involves data distributed across multiple mining sites, where visual distributions differ due to variations in equipment, illumination, coal flow conditions, camera viewpoints, and production batches. Centralized training requires transferring raw data from different mines, which raises concerns regarding communication cost, data privacy, and operational security. Although standard federated learning enables collaborative model training without sharing raw data, a single global model often struggles to adapt to mine-specific data distributions, especially under non-IID and class-imbalanced conditions. To address this problem, we propose FedMineMT, a personalized federated learning framework for coal mine foreign object detection. FedMineMT adopts a shared-encoder and personalized-head architecture: the server aggregates only the shared feature encoder to learn cross-mine visual representations, while each client preserves its own classification head to capture local mining-site characteristics. In addition, each client maintains a local exponential moving average teacher model, which provides a stable representation of historical local training states. During local optimization, FedMineMT combines the standard cross-entropy loss with a KL-divergence consistency regularization between the current student model and the local EMA teacher. This design prevents the client model from being overly biased by transient local batches and improves training stability under changing production conditions. Experiments are conducted on a coal mine image classification dataset consisting of three mining-site clients. We compare FedMineMT with representative federated baselines, including FedAvg and FedProx, using overall accuracy, client-level accuracy, class-wise accuracy, and convergence curves as evaluation metrics. The results demonstrate that FedMineMT improves personalized detection performance and enhances robustness across heterogeneous mining sites, offering a privacy-preserving and locally adaptive solution for federated foreign object detection in coal mine production environments.
