AB  - The rapid urbanization in coastal areas has exacerbated the conflict among Production-Living-Ecological (PLE) spaces, posing a challenge to the sustainable development of land and sea. This study builds a data-intensive analysis process that integrates multi-time phase remote sensing, spatial modeling and policy text mining into a unified computing workflow, so as to quantitatively evaluate the long-term PLE spaces evolution and policy impact of the Fishing Port Economic Zone (FPEZ). Taking the Weitou Fishing Port Area in Jinjiang City, China as an example, the process systematically extracts, classifies and models the space-time transformation of production, living and ecological functions, and reveals how policy intervention can reshape the land and sea spatial structure. The framework effectively quantifies dynamic changes and provides support for evidence-based spatial decision-making in the integrated management of the coastal zone. Through the interdisciplinary cooperation of spatial data science and environmental planning, this research has promoted the intelligent decision-making of land-sea coordinated governance. It demonstrates the applicability of advanced data science methods in spatial governance and provides a scalable reference for integrating data analysis into sustainable coastal management and ecologically efficient planning.
