AB  - Search engine optimization (SEO) plays a critical role in enhancing website visibility and user engagement, where bounce rate serves as a key indicator of content relevance and user satisfaction. However, predicting bounce rates accurately remains challenging due to data scarcity and classification uncertainty. This research proposes an Uncertainty-aware Consensus-based Classification Enhancement (UCCE) framework designed to improve the accuracy and reliability of webpage bounce rate classification. The study introduces a multi-website dataset integrating on-page SEO features with engagement metrics collected from seven bilingual (Arabic and English) websites. The proposed approach optimized the breaking-ties uncertainty metric and combined uncertainty quantification with a consensus voting mechanism among multiple classifiers, enabling correction of high-uncertainty predictions without retraining the main model. Experiments across nine machine learning classifiers identified Gradient Boosting Trees (GBT) as the primary model, achieving baseline accuracy of 64.71%. Applying UCCE yielded an average accuracy improvement of + 2.46%, while modifying only 1.06% of predictions, demonstrating high correction efficiency. The results validate UCCE as a scalable, selective, and generalizable enhancement framework for classification tasks affected by uncertainty.
