AB  - Few-shot image classification, which aims to recognize novel classes according to a few labeled samples, has received much attention recently in the remote sensing field. However, many existing methods in literature fail to work in the absence of human annotations, and additionally, their performances are generally limited due to both the high inter-class similarity and intra-class variation among the input remote sensing images. To address these challenges, we propose HiGAM (Hierarchical Graph Memory with Adaptive Temperature Control), an unsupervised meta-learning framework that learns transferable representations from unlabeled base images through contrastive pseudo-task construction. Specifically, a Multi-Level Residual Fusion module integrates features from different network depths to improve representation quality. A Hierarchical Graph Memory Bank then organizes the evolving feature memory through an HNSW-indexed graph, applies similarity-aware memory replacement, and supports Sinkhorn-guided pseudo-support construction and graph-conditioned hard-negative mining. Finally, an Adaptive Temperature Control mechanism dynamically adjusts the effective contrastive temperature according to batch-wise similarity statistics. We evaluate HiGAM on five remote sensing benchmarks, including NWPU, WHU-RS19, UCM, AID, and EuroSAT, as well as two general few-shot datasets, MiniImageNet and Omniglot. Experimental results show that HiGAM outperforms most compared label-free methods under the reported settings and remains competitive with several supervised approaches, without requiring base-class annotations.
