AB  - The vertical column densities (VCDs) of total nitrogen dioxide (NO2) and tropospheric formaldehyde (HCHO) in the atmosphere serve as core foundational elements for regional environmental planning management, air quality monitoring and control, and atmospheric environmental mechanism research. While satellite remote sensing technology enables long-term, large-scale observations, factors such as cloud and rain obscuration result in extensive data gaps ranging from 15% to 60%, severely limiting the analysis of the spatiotemporal patterns of pollution. To address the spatial data gaps in the VCD products of NO2 and HCHO using satellite data from the tropospheric monitoring instrument (TROPOMI), this article focuses on the Fenwei Plain in China and proposes a reconstruction method for atmospheric remote sensing products based on spatiotemporal adaptive block tensor completion. By mining the spatiotemporal neighborhood similarities and interannual periodic characteristics of pollutant VCDs, this article constructs a three-dimensional (3-D) spatiotemporal tensor with low-rank characteristics. It further introduces an adaptive mechanism that dynamically allocates weights based on the low-rank property of each dimension to achieve seamless spatiotemporal reconstruction of TROPOMI NO2 and HCHO VCDs over the Fenwei Plain from 2019 to 2023. The results demonstrate that this approach elevates the spatiotemporal coverage of both atmospheric pollutant VCD datasets to 100%. Even when the NO2 data gap reaches 50%, a high reconstruction accuracy is maintained (the coefficient of determination reaches 0.90). When the HCHO data gap reaches 30%, the reconstruction accuracy is also satisfactory (the coefficient of determination reaches 0.83) while improving the computational efficiency by 69%–84%. This method not only meets the trace gas data needs for regional pollution prevention and control but also provides a technical paradigm for remote sensing data reconstruction, with significant strategic and methodological value.
