AB  - Understanding the interaction of climate, topography, soil, and anthropogenic factors on remote sensing ecological index (RSEI) is helpful to reveal the feedback mechanism of mining disturbance in response to environmental conditions and climate change. Based on the data extracted by Google Earth Engine, the direct and indirect influences of climate, terrain, soil, and anthropogenic factors on RSEI in Yuzhong Mining area of Henan Province were quantitatively analyzed by partial least squares model. The results showed that: 1) In the application of time series monitoring in mining areas, the average RSEI values over the five years were 0.56, 0.38, 0.56, 0.58, and 0.67, respectively, and the overall ecological environment showed a trend of first declining and then rising; 2) from 2000 to 2010, the degradation rates of ecological environment were 31.5% and 21.7%, respectively. However, from 2010 to 2020, ecological conditions have improved significantly, mainly in the cultivated land and grassland areas around the mining area; 3) climate, terrain, soil, and anthropogenic factors have a positive influence on RSEI distribution, while anthropogenic variables have a negative influence on RSEI distribution, showing an N-type trend. This article clarifies the complex interaction between natural environmental conditions and mining activities in the formation of ecological quality, thereby providing a scientific basis for monitoring, managing, and restoring ecosystems affected by mining under constantly changing climate and anthropogenic pressure.
