AB  - Single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD) arrays produce dense kilohertz streams of photon-count frames, yet under high-speed capture most frames are invalid: the sub-millisecond exposure often catches a moving target just entering or leaving the field of view-or no target at all-and blindly feeding such blank or truncated frames misleads downstream task models. We address keyframe mining from high-speed photon-count streams: frame-level validity filtering before transmission. We present a controlled physical benchmark that reproduces these entry-exit dynamics- digit targets on a five-blade chopper at 520 Hz (31,200 RPM), captured by a 32 × 32 SPAD array at 2.5 kHz-and Nano Gatekeeper, a self-supervised Siamese ranking network trained on physics-based degradation synthesis without manual labels. With 271k parameters, it attains 97.91% mean average precision on real captures in which only 382 of 1,059 annotated frames (36.1%) are valid, runs at 2,501 FPS on an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano, and boosts downstream classification accuracy over existing baselines from 30% to 80% at a Top-1% transmission budget, and from 31% to 61% at Top-10%.
