TI  - Hyperspectral Remote Sensing Monitoring of Soil Heavy Metal Pollution in Rare Earth Mining Areas Based on Multivariate Empirical Mode Decomposition
JO  - IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Applied Earth Observations and Remote Sensing
PY  - 2026
VL  - 19
PB  - IEEE
DO  - 10.1109/JSTARS.2026.3702016
SN  - 2151-1535
AB  - Rare earth (RE) elements are vital strategic resources. However, prolonged use of extraction techniques such as pond leaching, heap leaching, and in-situ leaching, in which acidic solutions are used to displace RE ions, has led to severe soil acidification. This process further promotes heavy metal mobilization, causing significant contamination in mining areas, degrading the soil environment, and posing serious health risks to surrounding communities. Under such pollution stress, vegetation in mining areas may experience heavy metal stress, resulting in growth inhibition and altered spectral characteristics. Hyperspectral technology can detect subtle stress-induced changes in vegetation spectra, offering the potential to indirectly estimate soil heavy metal pollution through the spectral signatures of mining-area vegetation. This study focused on the abandoned Keshutang RE mine in Xunwu County, Ganzhou City, where 55 paired Camellia oleifera leaf and soil samples were collected. Leaf hyperspectral data were acquired using an ASD FieldSpec 4 spectrometer over the wavelength range of 350–2500 nm, and soil heavy metal concentrations were determined after acid digestion using inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry. Due to the relatively weak spectral response of Camellia oleifera to heavy metal stress, several spectral transformation techniques, including first- and second-order derivatives, continuum removal, and multivariate empirical mode decomposition (MEMD), were applied to enhance the original spectra and improve their correlation with soil heavy metal concentrations. The results showed that these transformations significantly strengthened the correlation between Camellia oleifera spectra and the concentrations of heavy metals such as Pb and Cd. On this basis, partial least squares regression and random forest regression models were developed. Compared with traditional spectral preprocessing methods, among the MEMD-based random forest regression models, the IMF1 component showed the highest accuracy and stability in predicting heavy metal concentrations. In addition, soil Pb and Cd concentrations exceeded the relevant regulatory thresholds, indicating contamination in terms of total concentrations. These findings provide a new technical approach for rapid screening and risk assessment of soil heavy metal pollution in RE mining areas, and may support ecological restoration monitoring and environmental management.
