AB  - This study tackles large-gradient 3-D surface deformation monitoring in mining areas, where InSAR suffers coherence loss and pixel offset tracking struggles with accuracy–efficiency balance. We propose a novel 3-D displacement retrieval algorithm integrating the Farneback optical flow (FOF) method with coordinated ascending and descending observations from China’s LuTan-1 (LT-1) SAR satellites. The approach first uses FOF to efficiently extract 2-D line-of-sight and azimuth displacement fields from single-track SAR imagery. Exploiting the quasi-synchronous ascending–descending capability of the LT-1 dual-satellite system, a rigorous geometric model is constructed to resolve the ill-posed 3-D deformation problem by combining multi-dimensional independent observations. An overdetermined system of equations is solved to robustly separate vertical, east–west, and north–south components. Tests at an Inner Mongolia coal mine show vertical displacements agree well with leveling, achieving an RMSE of 0.224 m and R2 of 0.949. Compared with the adaptive cross-correlation window POT algorithm, the proposed method improves RMSE and mean absolute error by 7.82% and 4.83%, respectively, while cutting computation time from several hours to about 10 seconds. Using nearly one year of SAR image sequences, the dynamic 3-D displacement field is reconstructed, revealing maximum displacements over 2.5 m and spatiotemporal propagation patterns. The integrated “FOF + multi-geometry SAR cooperative observation” algorithm effectively overcomes coherence loss, limited accuracy, computational complexity, and ill-posed inversion, providing a reliable technical pathway for high spatiotemporal-resolution vector deformation fields and highlighting LT-1’s potential for high-precision engineering deformation monitoring.
