AB  - Forests store half of the world’s terrestrial carbon, which regulates the biosphere and climate. However, industrialisation and mining have disrupted forest productivity. This study examines changes in high-density vegetation (HDV) over five years (2019-2023) in the IndoGangetic plain (Sahibganj, Jharkhand) using NDVI indices. The study focuses on the relationship between chlorophyll and vegetation indices in forests with slopes $\geq$ and ${\lt}15^{\circ}$. Results show a significant decline in HDV from $140.97 \mathrm{~km}^{2}$ to 133.86 $\mathrm{km}^{2}$. The correlation between MCARI and NDVI, and $\mathrm{CI}_{\mathrm{RE}}$ and NDVI, was highest on steeper slopes, with the ordinary least squares (OLS) regression model performing best for slopes $\geq 15^{\circ}$ (R of 0.81 and 0.80 for $\mathrm{CI}_{\mathrm{RE}}$ and MCARI, respectively). These findings highlight the potential of $\mathrm{CI}_{\mathrm{RE}}$ and MCARI for estimating chlorophyll content in higher terrain.
