AB  - Excessive exploitation of coal resources in mining areas has caused surface subsidence, leading to the deterioration of the ecological environment. InSAR is a key technology for monitoring surface deformation in mining areas. However, during mining, the surface subsidence is three-dimensional, and InSAR can only measure one-dimensional deformation in the direction of the satellite's line of sight. This limitation affects the integrity of surface deformation monitoring in mining areas. This paper adopts an InSAR three-dimensional deformation extraction method combined with mining subsidence knowledge to extract three-dimensional surface deformation. By utilizing the proportional relationship between the horizontal displacement caused by underground mining and the vertical subsidence gradient, combined with the spatial relationship involved in InSAR technology, a three-dimensional deformation solution model is constructed. A coal mine in Inner Mongolia Autonomous Region was used as the study area, and two-view Sentinel-1 satellite data were used for experimental verification. The results show that this method can obtain the three-dimensional deformation field of the surface in the study area, and the inversion results reveal the real surface deformation characteristics of the area, which provides important application value for monitoring surface deformation characteristics and exploring subsidence laws during mining in mining areas.
